Here is a complete example of a 7th-order mixed \(\pm n\) master guide curve spanning a 625 × 625 unit canvas, with six discrete anchor points marked along the spine where repeatable units (like small crosses, diamonds, or fleurons) sit.

1. The Mixed \(\pm n\) Spine Equation

To keep the horizontal spacing uniform across a 625-unit grid while letting the vertical profile arch smoothly and decelerate without a runaway tail, we drive X with a pure linear span (\(x(t) = 625t\)) and drive Y with alternating positive and negative \(n\) values scaled to \(625\):
\(y(t)=625\left(1.0t-0.5t^{2}+0.5t^{3}-0.5t^{4}+0.3t^{5}-0.2t^{6}+0.1t^{7}\right)\)
• Why It Stays Graceful: The positive lower-order terms (\(t, t^3, t^5, t^7\)) lift the spine steadily upward, while the negative even-order terms (\(-0.5t^2, -0.5t^4, -0.2t^6\)) gently dampen the acceleration so the slope flattens from a near-diagonal \(1/1\) start down to a calm \(0.5/1\) glide at the tip.
• Exact Factorial Multipliers: In raw control-vector form (\(\mathbf{u}_k = n_k \cdot 625 \cdot k!\)), the 5th-, 6th-, and 7th-order vertical inputs are \(+22,500\), \(-90,000\), and \(+315,000\).

